abstract:
JRD-001 develops the theory of judgment-relative descent: when mathematical information may be compressed, quotiented, erased, or transported without changing the judgments that matter within a declared jurisdiction. Its foundational principle is: what can be forgotten depends on which judgments must survive. The canonical v1.0 theorem packet begins from a representation R:X→YR:X	o Y and a declared judgment J:X→AJ:X	o A. It characterizes when JJ descends through RR—that is, when there exists a judgment J^:Y→A\widehat J:Y	o A such that J=J^∘R.J=\widehat J\circ R. Equivalently, a judgment descends exactly when it is constant on the fibers of the representation, or in kernel language, ker⁡R⊆ker⁡J.\ker R\subseteq\ker J. For a family of judgments J\mathcal J, the resulting semantic kernel determines which distinctions may be erased while preserving every judgment in the declared consumer language. JRD-001 then separates semantic sufficiency from lawful admissibility. Two representations may preserve the same extensional verdicts while differing in whether they preserve the witness, proof, strategy, authority, scope, or other structure required for a lawful continuation. Semantic equivalence therefore does not by itself authorize unrestricted quotienting or transport. The packet develops: judgment-relative factorization criteria; canonical semantic kernels and quotient constructions; lawful-versus-semantic quotient separation; maximal and greatest lawful-kernel results under stated closure hypotheses; effective assembly of compatible local descent data; quotient normalization conditions; typed reflection principles and counterexamples; and finite separation courts distinguishing judgment preservation from stronger forms of preservation. Its reflection results establish an important hierarchy: judgment descent⇏witness reflection⇏proof reflection⇏strategy reflection⇏authority reflection.	ext{judgment descent} 
ot\Rightarrow 	ext{witness reflection} 
ot\Rightarrow 	ext{proof reflection} 
ot\Rightarrow 	ext{strategy reflection} 
ot\Rightarrow 	ext{authority reflection}. Accordingly, preserving an answer does not automatically preserve the mathematical structure that produced, justified, operationalized, or authorized that answer. Within the Continuity Mathematics canon, JRD-001 provides a general framework for determining which distinctions are dispensable relative to a declared consumer and which apparently redundant distinctions remain mathematically or lawfully necessary. This deposit preserves mathematical standing v1.0 as the controlling Canonical Theorem Packet and constitutes Publication Release R3. Mathematical standing and publication release are distinct coordinates. R3 introduces: research_delta: NONE authority_delta: NONE claim_state_delta: NONE standing_delta: NONE The recovered predecessor contains earlier v1.0-RC1 integration material together with the later canonical v1.0 release. The controlling supersession record states that v1.0 controls and that v1.0-RC1 is superseded integration history. Publication Release R3 therefore preserves the canonical v1.0 theorem packet and does not reactivate or elevate the earlier RC1 integration layer. Verified public predecessor DOI:10.5281/zenodo.21816145 Historical R2 target DOI:10.5281/zenodo.21844296 The historical R2 identifier is retained in the provenance record, but its actual publication status was not independently established in the current R3 audit. R3 therefore does not assert a stronger public-version relationship than the evidence supports. Current DOI — Publication Release R3:10.5281/zenodo.22238129 The authoritative predecessor carrier and canonical release were recovered and integrity-verified. All 10 SHA-1 entries declared for the canonical release folder independently passed verification. The governed canonical theorem source is preserved through an explicit CRP-TEXT serialization relation. The public source differs from the governed Box source only by the single terminal line feed required by CRP-TEXT 1.0; removing that terminal LF reproduces the governed source byte-for-byte. Public canonical source SHA-256:0a3eea4f0772a686b4215ed1aed01902a3a75155288c0d7dda6bff3b47ee45f9 Governed source SHA-256:d0816bff1b345de81de2543c4ebb3d4fee40a864b6aab908577bb91ac80d81f0 R3 is a preservation-style MIG-P migration under CRP v3.3 / Zenodo Profile v1.1. Its exact conformance claim is: CRP-3.3:CRP-PROFILE-ZENODO-RELEASE-001@1.1 The R3 publication layer adds deterministic archival identity and certification without altering the mathematics. It includes: reconstructible research, world, and certification horizons; an immutable release cut; version-pinned consequential influence records; CRP-CJSON and CRP-TEXT canonical serialization; deterministic closed-world ZIP construction; independent byte-for-byte double-build verification; exact eight-member external control synchrony; a canonical SHA-256 checksum; and an external seal binding the final publication object. The final R3 release contains exactly 8 declared public members. Canonical sealed R3 archive SHA-256:b06fb9158309cf9fbb6f1a8804940a1063bf96fdf288fac4bc3f245a9d9b8615 This digest identifies the exact deterministic Publication Release R3 object. The predecessor canonical packet records internal proof audit and finite replay as passing while leaving external specialist review and bibliography/priority review open. Those open boundaries remain open. R3 does not claim that repository publication, deterministic sealing, protocol conformance, DOI assignment, or internal verification establishes novelty priority, external peer review, or mathematical authority beyond the canonical JRD-001 v1.0 claim ceiling. JRD-001 also carries no newly asserted reuse license in R3. The authenticated source and recovered R2 carrier did not establish an explicit license, so Publication Release R3 preserves: relicensing: false and does not invent rights that were absent from the controlling evidence. Publication, deterministic sealing, checksum verification, release-cut reconstruction, repository deposit, and DOI assignment establish archival identity, provenance, and reproducibility. They do not enlarge theorem scope, close open external review or priority questions, or create authority beyond the hypotheses, evidence, jurisdiction, and claim ceiling of canonical JRD-001 v1.0. Kelly Rhys — Origin Witness

